---
/**
 * A control for something the site will do and does not do yet.
 *
 * Used twice in the navbar — Accounts and Downloads — and once more inside the
 * body of the pages those two would have led to. The rule it encodes: a
 * disabled control must say what it is for. A greyed-out button with no
 * explanation is a dead end that looks like a bug.
 *
 * ## No JavaScript
 *
 * `popover` gives the panel a top layer, `Escape`, and light dismiss with no
 * script at all — the same platform feature `CitePreview` is built on
 * (ADR-0024), used here in its simplest form. `title` carries the short version
 * for a pointer that hovers without clicking, so the two mechanisms the request
 * asked for — hover text and a pop-up — are both present and neither costs a
 * kilobyte.
 *
 * ## Neither `disabled` nor `aria-disabled`
 *
 * A genuinely disabled button is removed from the tab order and refuses focus,
 * so a keyboard reader cannot reach it and a screen reader will not announce
 * it — which would hide the explanation from exactly the people least able to
 * guess at it.
 *
 * `aria-disabled="true"` was the first attempt and is also wrong, which the
 * Playwright suite caught by refusing to click it: "element is not enabled".
 * That refusal is correct, and it is the point. The button is *not* disabled —
 * it has an action, it performs it, and it performs it every time. What is
 * unavailable is the feature it names, and saying "this control does nothing"
 * about a control that does something is a lie told to exactly the readers who
 * cannot see the greyed-out styling.
 *
 * So it is an ordinary enabled button whose action is to explain itself. The
 * unavailability is carried by things that are true: the word "soon" beside the
 * label, the sentence in `title`, and a visually-hidden phrase for anyone who
 * gets neither. That is why the greyed look lives in `.soon__trigger` rather
 * than in `:disabled`.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Unique on the page: ties the trigger to its panel. */
  id: string;
  /** What the control would be called if it worked. */
  label: string;
  /** The panel's heading. */
  heading: string;
  /** The sentence a hover shows, and the panel's first paragraph. */
  lead: string;
  /** The second paragraph — context, or where to go instead. */
  detail?: string;
  /** `nav` renders it as a navbar item; `inline` as a standalone block. */
  variant?: "nav" | "inline";
}

const { id, label, heading, lead, detail, variant = "nav" } = Astro.props;
const panelId = `${id}-panel`;
---

<span class={`soon soon--${variant}`}>
  <button
    class="soon__trigger"
    type="button"
    popovertarget={panelId}
    title={lead}
  >
    <span class="soon__label">{label}</span>
    <span class="soon__tag" aria-hidden="true">soon</span>
    <span class="usa-sr-only">— not available yet; select to read what it will do</span>
  </button>

  <div class="soon__panel" id={panelId} popover role="dialog" aria-labelledby={`${id}-h`}>
    <h2 class="soon__heading" id={`${id}-h`}>{heading}</h2>
    <p class="soon__lead">{lead}</p>
    {detail && <p class="soon__detail">{detail}</p>}
    <slot />
    {/* The panel is light-dismissable and closes on Escape, but a pointer user
        who opened it by clicking looks for a way to close it by clicking. */}
    <button class="usa-button usa-button--outline soon__close" type="button" popovertarget={panelId} popovertargetaction="hide">
      Close
    </button>
  </div>
</span>
